From 9a4312bf71f64a8cdcd371b2c846663fcbbef74f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 6 <phase6@openwch.local>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 02:50:38 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] riscv: accept the WCH QingKe 'xw' -march spelling

The stock parser rejects every -march string WCH's own EVT projects use:
riscv_parse_arch_string () stops after the 'c' extension, so rv32imacxw,
rv32imafcxw, rv32gcxw and rv32ecxw all die with "unsupported ISA substring
'xw'", while the official WCH 8.2.0 build accepts them.  Consume an optional
bare 'x' and then an optional 'w' as two independent steps after the
compressed-extension step, matching that build.

Spec source, all first-hand measurements of the official binary
ref/gcc/darwin-arm64/8.2.0/bin/riscv-none-embed-gcc,
  sha256 9d693fae19e3c9aa52e57683612235571e7c91ce7f1d0025b053df728f4fa14f:

- Archived per-case matrix, tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/probes/raw/
  01-march-gcc (32 rows = 14 -c invocations + the same 14 with
  -print-multi-directory + 4 negative cases).  Accepted: m02 rv32imacxw,
  m07 rv32gcxw, m08 rv32imafdcxw, m10 rv32ecxw.  Rejected, with the residual
  substring naming exactly how far the parser got: m03 rv32imac_xw -> '_xw',
  m04 rv32imacxw1p0 -> '1p0', m05 rv32imacxw2p0 -> '2p0', neg-xq
  rv32imac_xq -> '_xq', neg-xw9p9 rv32imac_xw9p9 -> '_xw9p9', neg-qq
  rv32imacqq -> 'qq'.  The underscore forms are not consumed, and no version
  suffix is accepted.
- tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/optsweep/optsweep.md section 4.3, which
  places the insertion point from those residuals: rv32imacxq reports 'q'
  (the 'x' was eaten) and rv32imacxwz reports 'z' (the 'xw' was eaten), so
  both characters are consumed immediately after the compressed-extension
  step and the parser is not matching the two-character string "xw" as a
  unit.  Those two cases do not by themselves decide whether the 'w' step is
  nested inside the 'x' step or sits beside it: the two readings behave
  identically on every input that contains an 'x', which is all this matrix
  holds.  The next item is what decides it.
- The deciding axis is a 'w' with no preceding 'x', measured in
  tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s4/defect-fidelity/out/official/raw/
  18-march-w-axis (16 invocations, derived table waxis.tsv).  w01 rv32imacw
  and w02 rv32iw are accepted (-S, rc=0), and w01's assembly is byte-identical
  to the rv32imacxw and rv32imacx baselines w05 and w06, so the 'w' step does
  not depend on an 'x' having been consumed: the two steps are parallel, not
  nested.  w03 rv32imacwx is rejected, so the 'w' step runs after the 'x' step
  rather than before it, and w04 rv32imacww is rejected, so it runs once.  The
  assembler is stricter than the compiler on the bare form -- w01c and w02c
  fail with "Fatal error: -march=rv32imacw: unsupported ISA subset `w'", which
  is the rejection the comment in the hunk records.
- 21 further live cases recorded in tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s3/d1/
  spec-probe/official-placement.txt, which fix the remaining position and
  multiplicity questions: rv32imaxw, rv32ixw, rv32gxw and rv32exw are all
  accepted, so the step is not conditional on 'c' having been consumed;
  rv32imacxx reports 'x' and rv32imacxww reports 'w', so each step runs once
  rather than in a loop; rv64imacxw and rv64gcxw are accepted too, so rv32
  and rv64 share the one code path.

No subset registration and no code-generation effect: the hunk touches
neither *flags nor any subset list, so -march=rv32imacxw leaves target_flags
exactly as -march=rv32imac leaves them.  Measured on the official binary
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/optsweep/optsweep.md section 4.4 and
tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s3/d1/spec-probe/official-extra.txt): -O2 -S
output is bytewise identical between rv32imac and rv32imacxw, and between
rv32imafc and rv32imafcxw; the XW code-size win appears only in the .o, i.e.
it belongs to the assembler.  Multilib selection needs no change either --
gcc/config/riscv/t-elf-multilib already lists march=rv32imacxw,
march=rv32imafcxw and march=rv32ecxw, and those entries match on the driver's
option text, which now survives parsing: rv32imacxw -> rv32imacxw/ilp32,
rv32ecxw -> rv32ecxw/ilp32e, rv32imafcxw -> rv32imafcxw/ilp32f, rv32gcxw and
rv32imafdcxw -> "." (7 values recorded in official-extra.txt).  The string
reaches the assembler untouched, as -march=rv32imacxw on the as command line
(tmp/toolchain_8.2.0/evidence/s1/probes/raw/03-attributes/driver-v.stderr).
---
 gcc/common/config/riscv/riscv-common.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/gcc/common/config/riscv/riscv-common.c b/gcc/common/config/riscv/riscv-common.c
index 8a78aebc3..d239e2037 100644
--- a/gcc/common/config/riscv/riscv-common.c
+++ b/gcc/common/config/riscv/riscv-common.c
@@ -114,6 +114,18 @@ riscv_parse_arch_string (const char *isa, int *flags, location_t loc)
   if (*p == 'c')
     *flags |= MASK_RVC, p++;
 
+  /* Accept the WCH QingKe vendor spelling, which appends an optional bare
+     'x' and then an optional 'w' as two independent steps (rv32imacxw,
+     rv32imacx and rv32imacw all pass; the assembler still rejects the bare
+     'w' form).  The string selects a multilib and is handed to the
+     assembler unchanged; no subset is registered and no target flag is set
+     here, so it has no effect on code generation.  */
+  if (*p == 'x')
+    p++;
+
+  if (*p == 'w')
+    p++;
+
   if (*p)
     {
       error_at (loc, "-march=%s: unsupported ISA substring %qs", isa, p);
-- 
